            <ArticleTitle>miR-21 is involved in transforming growth factor β1-induced chemoresistance and invasion by targeting PTEN in breast cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>6929-6936</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3892/ol.2017.7007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Transforming growth factor β1 (TGF-β1) has been associated with poor outcomes in patients with breast cancer. However, the functions and underlying molecular mechanisms of TGF-β1 in breast cancer remain unknown. Therefore, the present study aimed to identify the effects of components of the TGF-β/microRNA (miR-)21/phosphatase and tensin homolog (PTEN) signaling axis in breast cancer. TGF-β1 was identified to upregulate the expression of miR-21, and miR-21 was demonstrated to be significantly upregulated in breast cancer tissues compared with benign proliferative breast disease. In addition, the expression of miR-21 was significantly associated with increased TGF-β1 and clinical characteristics in patients, including tumor grade and lymph node metastasis (all P&lt;0.05). Furthermore, in the breast cancer MCF-7 cell line, TGF-β1 was revealed to induce the expression of miR-21 in a dose- and time-dependent manner. The results of the present study additionally demonstrated that increased miR-21, in response to TGF-β1 signaling, was associated with tumor invasion and chemoresistance in vitro. In addition, suppression of PTEN was mediated by TGF-β1-induced expression of miR-21 in breast cancer cells and using a miR-21 inhibitor revitalized the expression of PTEN. The results of the present study explored the functions of TGF-β1-stimulated expression of miR-21 to suppress the PTEN axis, which promotes breast cancer progression and chemoresistance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
